WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.000
You are trying to log in to a website. The domain&nbsp;
looks good - you opened it like you always do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.920
You think you typed your password correctly, but&nbsp;
the app tells you didn't it so you retype it again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:19.040
- it happens. But little do you know that by doing&nbsp;
that you just gave your passwords to the attacker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:26.880
Hello, the vulnerability I will show today is the&nbsp;
most fun and creative exploit I have ever done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:32.480
for bug bounty. I chained SSRF with phishing,&nbsp;
which doesn't really sound familiar, does it?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:38.160
Unfortunately, even though I'm very happy with&nbsp;
this bug, I still didn't make the most out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:44.720
it and learned the lesson the hard way. But for&nbsp;
now, enjoy the explanation! I found this bug in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:51.520
the private program. Let's say it's redacted.com.&nbsp;
There is a way to set up two factor authentication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:58.320
via Duo. The way it works is you create an&nbsp;
account on Duo. It generates credentials for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:06.480
you and assigns a subdomain that looks&nbsp;
like this: api-random.duosecurity.com.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.472
To set it up on redacted.com, you copy these&nbsp;
credentials and the hostname. Then, redacted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.472 --> 00:01:22.000
does the validation. It checks if the host starts&nbsp;
with api- and ends with .duosecurity.com. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:32.400
these validations are robust. If you do them on&nbsp;
the host. But redacted.com did not. It didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:39.920
normalize the address and these validations were&nbsp;
run on your whole input, not just on the host.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:45.120
So it's quite easy. You can use this address to&nbsp;
bypass it while still controlling the hostname.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:53.280
And after the validation, redacted.com makes&nbsp;
a request to auth/v2/check endpoint. It just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:59.840
concatenates your host to this path and this is&nbsp;
the final location where your request is sent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:07.680
This way you can trick the server into sending&nbsp;
requests to an arbitrary location. It's a typical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:12.800
server-side request forgery. As you could&nbsp;
not read the response, it's a blind one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:20.320
And of course, after this request, redacted threw&nbsp;
an error because my service was not responding to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:27.520
this request with the expected format. But since&nbsp;
it's a blind SSRF anyway, it doesn't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:34.160
matter - the request was already sent. Usually,&nbsp;
to elevate the impact of a blind SSRF, you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:39.760
to find another service in the internal network&nbsp;
which you can exploit with a single request.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:46.720
So the impact of this bug varies a lot. But&nbsp;
redacted is an open-source application which you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:54.560
can install yourself. So impact here is greater&nbsp;
because, you know, the company can say "we don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.560 --> 00:03:00.800
have any internal services that can be chained&nbsp;
with a blind SSRF", but they can't say "none of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:07.760
our clients has services in the internal network&nbsp;
that could be chained with it". So I reported the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:14.800
blind SSRF and went to sleep as it was quite late&nbsp;
and I'm not a night owl. But remember I told you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:21.040
earlier there was an error due to my server not&nbsp;
responding with the expected format? When I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:27.760
falling asleep, I was wondering what would happen&nbsp;
if my server would return a proper response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:35.040
just like a real Duo API server would. Maybe I&nbsp;
even dreamt about it, but I don't remember that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:43.520
So the next morning, I came back to it. I thought&nbsp;
that maybe if I managed to set up the integration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:49.840
with my server, something interesting will be sent&nbsp;
to me during the logging process. So I created a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:55.680
Duo account and used real credentials. And this&nbsp;
is the response that my proof of concept server&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:02.880
had to return. And it worked! I successfully&nbsp;
added two-factor authentication with my server&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:10.400
and the way the Duo integration works is that&nbsp;
after the login, the iframe from my subdomain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:16.240
is opened with the input field where you&nbsp;
fill in the OTP code that you received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:23.280
via a text message from Duo. So when I was&nbsp;
trying to log in, the server tried to open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:29.440
an iframe from my own server. It was a perfect&nbsp;
attack scenario. I created a proof of concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:36.400
that looked like redacted.com's login page.&nbsp;
And after the login, my form was shown in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:44.400
victim's browser. It looked exactly like the one&nbsp;
from redacted.com... minus my HTML/CSS skills.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:52.000
The domain of the iframe is not visible to the&nbsp;
victim, so this is pretty much perfect phishing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.000
In a real word attack, I could also add&nbsp;
the message about an invalid password.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:03.520
It's because the victim is taken to my&nbsp;
iframe after a successful login. I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:09.840
show them an alert about an invalid&nbsp;
password so that they retype it again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:17.520
But the question you might be asking yourself&nbsp;
is what is the impact of phishing myself? The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:24.480
thing is that redacted.com also has teams and as&nbsp;
the admin of the team, I can set up two-factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:30.560
authentication for all members. And with this&nbsp;
attack, I can get their plaintext passwords.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:37.840
This, however, makes the attack less likely.&nbsp;
So even though the attack is super cool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:44.880
is not as impactful. And this is a place I find&nbsp;
myself quite often when doing bug bounty. I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:51.360
like a childish curiosity - I just want to break&nbsp;
things and not care about the impact. I'm not sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:58.720
if that's a good thing because not always cool&nbsp;
attacks result in equally great impact. And I feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:05.120
that if I want to get maximum return on investment&nbsp;
from doing bug bounty, I should focus on things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:10.880
that are the most impactful and not necessarily&nbsp;
the coolest. But on the other hand, in some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:20.800
situations that curiosity is definitely helping me&nbsp;
out. This attack gave me so much satisfaction. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:27.440
I added the comment to the original report. One&nbsp;
problem was that the CSP didn't allow opening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:33.520
iframes from my domain. It reminded me of my&nbsp;
recent endeavours with the content security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:42.240
policy on Stripe. I didn't initially report my 2&nbsp;
XSSes because of the CSP. Later, turned out they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:50.240
rewarded me for them very well, treating CSP&nbsp;
only as a defence in-depth mechanism. So here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:57.040
I didn't consider turning off CSP a big problem.&nbsp;
But this program had a different attitude.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:05.440
They only rewarded me $500 which looked like a&nbsp;
reward that didn't consider the phishing attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:14.160
scenario - only the blind SSRF. So I replied&nbsp;
to them, saying why I think I should get more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:19.760
I mentioned a possible bypass of the first&nbsp;
fix and explained this second attack scenario.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:26.320
But unfortunately, by doing it, I spoiled&nbsp;
it and I'm still angry with myself for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.320
I thought about bypassing&nbsp;
the content security policy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:36.880
but it's an iframe and I only controlled the host,&nbsp;
so I couldn't think of anything worth trying here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:44.400
And for my augmentation, they gave me $500 more.&nbsp;
And initially, I was very happy with that reward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:49.520
But after a few days, when the bug&nbsp;
was fixed and the report was closed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:55.600
I logged in to redacted.com. But this&nbsp;
time not via the website, but with an app&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:02.480
and to my eyes, my phishing page popped up&nbsp;
without turning off the content security policy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:11.600
Turned out that my phishing exploit was complete,&nbsp;
just via another login channel. And in case of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:18.360
this app, I think it may be a more popular&nbsp;
way to log in than the website itself. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:23.040
I didn't know how much more they would&nbsp;
be willing to pay me, but definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:29.200
something more. But after already arguing one&nbsp;
time, I didn't want to do it the second time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:34.480
since the report was already closed. And&nbsp;
I'm still angry with myself for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:39.840
It's a hard way to learn to always,&nbsp;
always check different channels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:48.160
But even though I didn't mention it in the report,&nbsp;
I know that my proof of concept was complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:56.000
and this was the coolest exploit I have&nbsp;
ever done, at least so far. And after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:02.160
$1,000 is not bad, especially since found it&nbsp;
after just starting hunting on the program.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:09.600
With this money, I'm fulfilling one of&nbsp;
my dreams to go to an F1 race. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:15.120
right now, as this video is published, I'm in&nbsp;
Barcelona on Spanish Formula One Grand Prix.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:21.920
Less than a year ago I was a pentester&nbsp;
and even though I had web security skills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:27.840
I just couldn't find bugs in bug bounty. I was&nbsp;
learning a lot and I hoped that it will finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:34.400
yield results. Albeit bug bounty is not easy and&nbsp;
definitely not a get rich quick scheme, I can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:40.480
that all this learning pays off and I'm finding&nbsp;
cool bugs that enable me to fulfil my dreams.&nbsp;&nbsp;

